import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.G0y0PQA7.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Model menu","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/features/model.md","filePath":"en/features/model.md"}'),r={name:"en/features/model.md"};function s(i,e,d,l,g,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="model-menu" tabindex="-1">Model menu <a class="header-anchor" href="#model-menu" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Model menu&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The dialogs opened from the Model menu — word dictionary, groups and global groups, sequences, triggers and tablespaces, options, change history, translation management, test data and model properties. The same commands are also in canvas right-click › Document ▸.</p><p>Dialogs that edit objects on the canvas, such as tables, columns, views and relationships, are covered in <a href="/relio/en/features/dialogs">Properties dialogs</a>.</p><h2 id="menu-items" tabindex="-1">Menu items <a class="header-anchor" href="#menu-items" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Menu items&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Menu › Item</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Model › Category ▸</td><td>Manage categories…, Show referencing tables — <a href="/relio/en/features/categories">Categories</a></td></tr><tr><td>Model › Manage groups…</td><td>Column groups in this document</td></tr><tr><td>Model › Manage global groups…</td><td>Column groups shared by several files</td></tr><tr><td>Model › Word dictionary…</td><td>The word list and cleanup</td></tr><tr><td>Model › Change history…</td><td>Keep, view, compare and restore snapshots</td></tr><tr><td>Model › Translation management…</td><td>Translation dictionaries used by this document</td></tr><tr><td>Model › Test data…</td><td>Create and edit test data</td></tr><tr><td>Model › Add trigger… · Add sequence… · Add tablespace…</td><td>New objects (sequences and tablespaces only on databases that support them)</td></tr><tr><td>Model › Options…</td><td>Database and target version, tablespace environments, defaults for new tables, check options</td></tr><tr><td>Model › Model properties…</td><td>The items shown in the stamp</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="word-dictionary-and-word-properties" tabindex="-1">Word dictionary and word properties <a class="header-anchor" href="#word-dictionary-and-word-properties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Word dictionary and word properties&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The <strong>Word dictionary</strong> dialog lists the words and how many columns use each one (<strong>Used by columns</strong>). Filter with <strong>Find by name</strong>, and use <strong>Unused words only</strong> to see words no column uses. <strong>Select all unused words</strong> followed by <strong>Clean up unused words</strong> removes them all at once (can be undone). Double-click a row to open its word properties.</li><li>The <strong>Word properties</strong> dialog edits the physical name · logical name · type · length · scale · enum/set arguments · description. It also notes that edits apply to every column using the word, and lists those columns (double-click one to go to it on the canvas).</li></ul><h2 id="manage-groups-and-global-groups" tabindex="-1">Manage groups and global groups <a class="header-anchor" href="#manage-groups-and-global-groups" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Manage groups and global groups&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A group is a set of columns shared by several tables (for example common columns such as created date and modified by).</p><ul><li>In the group list on the left use <strong>Add</strong> · <strong>Edit</strong> · <strong>Delete</strong>; on the right, edit the <strong>Group name</strong> and the group column table (add, edit, delete, reorder, Quick entry), then click <strong>Update</strong>.</li><li>Editing a group applies to every table that uses it. Deleting a group that tables use lists those tables and asks whether to continue.</li><li><strong>Add to global groups</strong> copies a document group to this PC&#39;s global groups. <strong>Add to document</strong> in the Manage global groups dialog copies a global group into this document&#39;s groups (after that they are independent). If the name already exists, Relio offers <strong>Add with a new name</strong>.</li><li>When you create a new diagram, all global groups are added to the document&#39;s groups.</li></ul><h2 id="sequences-triggers-and-tablespaces" tabindex="-1">Sequences, triggers and tablespaces <a class="header-anchor" href="#sequences-triggers-and-tablespaces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sequences, triggers and tablespaces&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Create them by right-clicking in the outline, with <strong>+ Add</strong>, or from the Model menu, and edit them by double-clicking.</p><ul><li><strong>Sequence properties</strong> — Sequence name · Schema · Increment · Start · Min value · Max value · Cache (No cache) · Cycle (CYCLE) · Description. Depending on the database, Data type, Digits (DECIMAL only) and Order (ORDER) are added. Values beyond 64 bits are allowed. Sequences cannot be created on databases that do not support them (MySQL, SQLite, MS Access and others).</li><li><strong>Trigger properties</strong> — Trigger name · Schema · SQL (appended as-is after CREATE TRIGGER name) · Description. PostgreSQL does not allow a schema on trigger names, so the schema is left out in the DDL.</li><li><strong>Tablespace properties</strong> — Choose the <strong>Environment</strong> at the top and enter the <strong>Name</strong>. The fields below differ by database (MySQL data file, log file group and storage engine; Oracle data file, file size and autoextend; Db2 type, page size, management, containers and buffer pool; PostgreSQL location and owner; SQL Server file group name, file path and size). Attributes are stored separately per environment.</li></ul><h2 id="options" tabindex="-1">Options <a class="header-anchor" href="#options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Options&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Change document settings in Model › Options… (or the target database field at the left end of the status bar).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tab</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Database</td><td>The database, <strong>Target product</strong> and <strong>Target version</strong>. Changing the database tells you that settings specific to the other database are kept but not used by the new one, and asks you to confirm (can be undone). The target version is remembered on this PC only.</td></tr><tr><td>Tablespace environments</td><td>The environment list (for example &quot;Production DB&quot;) with <strong>Add</strong> · <strong>Edit</strong> · <strong>Delete</strong>. The last environment cannot be deleted, and deleting an environment also deletes its tablespace attributes.</td></tr><tr><td>Advanced settings</td><td>Defaults used when creating a new table (schema, tablespace; for MySQL also storage engine, character set and so on).</td></tr><tr><td>Options</td><td><strong>Switch IME mode automatically when focus moves</strong> · <strong>Check characters allowed in physical names</strong> · <strong>Use curves (Bezier)</strong> · <strong>Turn off validation</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="change-history" tabindex="-1">Change history <a class="header-anchor" href="#change-history" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Change history&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Model › Change history… keeps a copy of the whole current diagram, with the date, inside the document.</p><ul><li>Briefly describe what changed in <strong>Changes</strong> and click <strong>Add</strong>; select an entry to <strong>Update</strong> or <strong>Delete</strong> it.</li><li><strong>View the data at this date</strong> — Opens that snapshot in a read-only preview tab.</li><li><strong>Restore to this point</strong> — Replaces the document contents with that snapshot (can be undone).</li><li><strong>Compare changes</strong> — Opens the selected history entry (A) and the current document (B) in a comparison tab. <a href="/relio/en/features/git#comparison-view">Git and merging › Comparison view</a></li><li>Each history entry stores a copy of the whole diagram, so the file grows. The bottom of the dialog shows the number of entries and their approximate size.</li></ul><h2 id="translation-management" tabindex="-1">Translation management <a class="header-anchor" href="#translation-management" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Translation management&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In Model › Translation management…, turn on <strong>Use translation when importing</strong> and check the translation dictionaries this document should use (upper ones take priority). The dictionary list itself lives in this PC&#39;s app settings and is managed with <strong>Add dictionary file…</strong> · <strong>Manage dictionaries…</strong> · <strong>Remove from list</strong>. A translation dictionary file is a UTF-8 text file with one &quot;source,translation&quot; pair per line. It is used to fill logical names when importing from a database. <a href="/relio/en/features/preferences#translation-dictionary">Settings › Translation dictionary</a></p><h2 id="test-data" tabindex="-1">Test data <a class="header-anchor" href="#test-data" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Test data&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In the list on the left of Model › Test data…, use <strong>Add</strong> · <strong>Edit</strong> · <strong>Delete</strong> · <strong>Copy</strong> · <strong>Output</strong>. The right side shows the table names and record counts of the selected data. <strong>Output</strong> applies your edits first and then opens the <a href="/relio/en/features/export#test-data">test data export</a> dialog.</p><p>The <strong>Edit test data</strong> dialog:</p><ul><li><strong>Name</strong>, with <strong>All tables</strong> on the left ⇄ <strong>Tables to generate data for (record count)</strong> on the right (Add ▶ / ◀ Remove).</li><li><strong>Output order</strong> — Direct input → repeat input, or repeat input → direct input.</li><li><strong>Direct input</strong> tab — Enter values directly in the grid. Pasting works too.</li><li><strong>Repeat input</strong> tab — Set the record count and click a column header to set its <strong>Repeat condition</strong>: <strong>Repeat type</strong> (NULL · Format · Enumeration · Foreign key (values in the model)), <strong>Repeat count</strong> (how many rows share the same value), the format (<code>%</code> is replaced by a number), start · end · step, and enumerated values. Click a row in the preview to set <strong>Override</strong> values for that row only.</li></ul><h2 id="model-properties" tabindex="-1">Model properties <a class="header-anchor" href="#model-properties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Model properties&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open Model › Model properties… or double-click the stamp. Add and delete rows in the <strong>Item name</strong> · <strong>Value</strong> table (project name, model name, version, company name, author and so on) with <strong>New item</strong>. <strong>Created</strong> and <strong>Updated</strong> are filled in automatically when you save. Turn on View › Show stamp to show them as a table on the canvas.</p>',27)])])}const p=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,p as default};
